			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1536373">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Reply&nbsp;to&nbsp;spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I also said this over in Part VII's discussion thread, but it bears repeating: In my mind, Captain Gagarin wasn't so much a junior Soviet officer aware of the Foundation as a Foundation agent who was under cover as a junior Soviet officer. The Foundation poaches the brightest and best from wherever it can, but in order to do that, it has to have people in positions capable of scouting talent. Also, having Foundation agents hidden away in various organizations, governments, corporations, and militaries around the world would seem like a way to make it easier to handle situations wherever and whenever they arise.</p>
<p>As to the Foundation behind the Iron Curtain, the Foundation-Soviet relationship is rocky at best, and incredibly complicated. They cooperate occasionally, but just as often spy on each other and disrupt each other's operations. The KGB and GRU would certainly try and keep the Foundation from actively operating in the Soviet sphere of influence, but not to the extent as to forcibly expel anyone they knew to be associated with the Foundation. It'd be just as likely that Directorate 13 (KGB) and Division P (GRU) would try and use the Foundation against the other. Also, by the time this tale is set, it's well into Gorbachev's leadership, so the waters are even more muddied.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

