				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hornby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1259135); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1259135&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609882012" alt="Hornby" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1259135)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hornby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1259135); return false;" >Hornby</a></span> <span class="odate time_1344970392 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Aug 2012 18:53</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1537176">
									

<p>I'm glad you enjoyed it. :)</p>
<p>On the &quot;few errors needing cleanup&quot;, I'm sure I made a couple (and people have already commented on one or two of them, yourself included). Don't hesitate to PM me with stuff you think needs to be fixed. I always like hearing constructive criticism for improvement (I sometimes choose to respectfully ignore it, but that's an author's prerogative).</p>
<p>Also, both in reply to you, Rhett, and to everyone else who's complimented me on the realism and scope, <strong>thank you very much</strong>. Those were two of the story aspects I was aiming for in particular, and I am overjoyed people seem to think I delivered.</p>
<p>I generally enjoy Tom Clancy as a writer - I've been reading his stuff since I was in 5th grade - and his writing style has definitely influenced mine. He has a tendency to go off the rails into political diatribes from time to time, which I'm less fond of when they're not plot-critical, but nobody's perfect. Anyway, the descriptions of the explosions come from liking what he did in the chapter &quot;Three Shakes&quot; in <em>The Sum of All Fears</em> (good book, terrible movie), coupled with liking to watch the Mythbusters blow stuff up on high-speed film. I <em>could</em> have written descriptions that amounted to &quot;flash of light, ball of fire, shockwave, BOOM&quot;, had my goal been to write something where the explosions were part of an action sequence. Instead, I was attempting to recreate what amounted to a beautiful high-speed shot of the explosions in prose form, since I did not believe a faster pacing was necessary. Also, though the real-world Pan Am explosion was Semtex (probably) rather than anomalous exploding coins, the specifics in my description of how the plane broke up under the stresses involved are about as accurate as I could make them. (Horrifically, though all aboard perished <em>eventually</em>, some of the passengers survived long enough to fall the nearly nine and a half kilometers to the ground and die in the inferno <span style="text-decoration: underline;">on the ground</span> - a grisly fact I didn't include because it wasn't plot-relevant, as all the casualties significant to the plot died nearly instantly.) The peculiarities of the Pan Am explosion and the mechanics of a fuel-air bomb (both of which differ from what people normally think of as the results of an explosion) are, to me at least, rather interesting. Hence, the description.</p>
<p><sup>Of course, now it does sound like studying the mechanics of explosions is a passion of mine, even though I just think they're kind of interesting.</sup></p>

							</div>

								
					
